          %h3#AxionsAsKKReductionOfHigherGaugeFields Axions as KK-reduction of higher gauge fields
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            axion fields
            %em naturally
            arise as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK+compactification"} KK compactification
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"}> B-field
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-field"} C-field
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-fields"} RR-fields
          %p Notice that this means that axions in string theory are as in the output of the original Peccei–Quinn proposal, but do not actually make use of the Peccei–Quinn mechanism.
          %p
            As amplified in particular in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#SvrcekWitten06"} Svrcek-Witten 06
          %blockquote
            %p
              An obvious question about the axion hypothesis is how natural it really is. Why introduce a global PQ “symmetry” if it is not actually a symmetry? What is the sense in constraining a theory so that the classical Lagrangian possesses a certain symmetry if the symmetry is actually anomalous? It could be argued that the best evidence that PQ “symmetries” are natural comes from string theory, which produces them without any contrivance. … the string compactifications always generate PQ symmetries, often spontaneously broken at the string scale and producing axions, but sometimes unbroken. (
              %a{:href => "#SvrcekWitten06"}> Svrcek-Witten 06, pages 3-4
              )
          %p
            Similarly from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#ADDKM09"} ADDKM 09
          %blockquote
            %p
              However, at the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/effective+field+theory"} effective field theory
              level it is hard to judge how natural it is to have such a “fake” global PQ symmetry which is explicitly broken exclusively by QCD. Note, that in order not to spoil the solution to the strong CP problem all other sources of explicit PQ symmetry breaking should be at least 10 orders of magnitude down with respect to the potential generated by the QCD anomaly, and one may be especially cautious about the viability of such a proposal, given the common lore that global symmetries always get broken by quantum gravitational effects [12, 13, 14]. This makes it natural to inquire whether axions arise naturally in the most developed quantum theory of gravity—string theory. (
              %a{:href => "#ADDKM09"}> ADDKM 09, p. 3
              )
          %p
            The mechanism discussed in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#SvrcekWitten06"} Svrcek-Witten 06
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic strings
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II strings
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            effects included, etc.) share the following properties:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the axion field itself is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
                of one of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+fields"} higher gauge fields
                in string theory, the (twisted)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kalb-Ramond+field"} Kalb-Ramond field
                for heterotic string, the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
                for the type II string, or the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
                in 11-dimensional supergravity with
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-brane"} M-brane
                effects;
            %li
              %p
                accordingly the Peccei-Quinn periodic shift symmetry of the axion results arises as the
                = succeed "-" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+symmetry"} gauge symmetry
                that is the dimensional reduction of the
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mn 2
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^2 U(1)
                (heterotic
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi B
                        %mn 3
                      %mi U
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B^3 U(1)
                (11d
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-field"} C-field
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+symmetry"} higher gauge symmetry
                = succeed "-periodicity" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(1)
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization"} Dirac charge quantization
                for these fields;
            %li
              %p
                the axion
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction+term"} interaction term
                of the form
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∝
                      %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∧
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \propto a \langle F \wedge F\rangle
                arises as the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/double+dimensional+reduction"} double dimensional reduction
                of the self-interaction terms of these higher gauge fields:
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    in type II string theory it is a component of the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+WZW+term"} higher WZW term
                    on coincident
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-branes"} D6-branes
                    with a component
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo ∝
                            %msubsup
                              %mi A
                              %mn 3
                              %mi RR
                            %mo ∧
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                            %mi F
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi F
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \propto A_{3}^{RR} \wedge \langle F \wedge F\rangle
                %li
                  %p
                    for 11d C-fields it is the term
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∝
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                          %mi F
                          %mo ∧
                          %mi F
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \propto C_{3} \wedge \langle F \wedge F\rangle
                    induced by
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomaly+cancellation"} anomaly cancellation
                    for M-theory at conical singularities (as discussed at
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %em
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory+on+G%E2%82%82-manifolds"} M-theory on G₂-manifolds
                %li
                  %p
                    for heterotic string theory it arises from integrating out the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrange+multiplier"} Lagrange multiplier
                    that enforces the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+anomaly+cancellation"} Green-Schwarz anomaly cancellation
                    in 4d.
          %p We now discuss this in more detail:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#IntypeIIA"} In type IIA string theory
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#InHeteroticStringTheory"} In heterotic string theory
          %h4#IntypeIIA In type IIA string theory
          %p
            Consider
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+IIA+string+theory"} type IIA string theory
            in the guise of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
            supported in intersecting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-branes"} D6-branes
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            fills all of 4d
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+WZW+term"} higher WZW term
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+action+functional"} Green-Schwarz action functional
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-brane"} D6-brane
            is of the form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∝
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 7
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi RR
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi exp
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\propto \int_{X_7} A^RR \wedge \langle \exp(F_\nabla) \rangle
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mi RR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^RR
            denotes the collective inhomogenous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/background+field"} background
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_\nabla
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chan-Paton+gauge+field"} Chan-Paton gauge field
            on the D-brane. The
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \langle -\rangle
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Killing+form"} Killing form
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/invariant+polynomial"} invariant polynomial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trace"} trace
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+unitary+Lie+algebra"} special unitary Lie algebra
            regarded as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix+Lie+algebra"} matrix Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 7
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_7
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D6-brane"} D6-brane
          %p Hence one of the three non-vanishing summands in this expression is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∝
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 7
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mn 3
                    %mi RR
                  %mo ∧
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\propto \int_{X_7} A_3^{RR} \wedge \langle F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla\rangle
                  \\,.
          %p
            Now assuming a Kaluza-Klein ansatz
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 7
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi X
                    %mn 4
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi Y
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X_7 = X_4 \times Y_3
            with
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Y
                        %mn 3
                  %msubsup
                    %mi A
                    %mn 3
                    %mi RR
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \coloneqq \int_{Y_3} A_3^{RR}
          %p
            the effective axion field in 4d, then this term becomes the axion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∝
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi X
                        %mn 4
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\propto \int_{X_4} a \langle F_{\nabla} \wedge F_{\nabla}\rangle
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#SvrcekWitten06"}> Svrcek-Witten 06, around (6.9)
            )
          %h4#InHeteroticStringTheory In heterotic string theory
          %p
            In
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactified
            to 4d, the 4d
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#SvrcekWitten06"} Svrcek-Witten 06, starting p. 15
          %p
            This works as follows: By the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+anomaly+cancellation"} Green-Schwarz anomaly cancellation
            mechanism, then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/heterotic+string+theory"} heterotic string theory
            is a twisted 2-form field, whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            locally has in addition to the exact differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d B
            also a fundamental 3-form contribution, so that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %mi d
                  %mi B
                  %mo +
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H = d B + C
          %p
            (locally). Moreover, the differential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d H
            is constrained to be the Pontryagin 4-form of the gauge potential
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            (minus that of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+curvature"} Riemann curvature
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi H
                  %mo =
                  %mi tr
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo ∧
                    %msub
                      %mi F
                      %mo ∇
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d H = tr \left(F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Now suppose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactification"} KK-compactification
            to 4d has been taken care of, then this constraint may be implemented in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            by adding it to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrange+multiplier"} Lagrange multiplier
            \:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mi H
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mi H
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mtext kinetic action
                      %mrow
                        %mtext for B-field
                  %mo +
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mi a
                        %mrow
                          %mo (
                          %mi d
                          %mi H
                          %mo −
                          %mi tr
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mo ∇
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mo ∇
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo )
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mtext Green-Schwarz constraint
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S
                  \=
                  \\underset{
                  \\text{kinetic action} \atop \text{for B-field}
                  }{
                  \\underbrace{\int_X H \wedge \star H}
                  }
                  +
                  \\underset{
                  \\text{Green-Schwarz constraint}
                  }{
                  \\underbrace{
                  \\int_X a \left( d H - tr(F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla) \right)
                  }
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Then, by the usual argument, one says that instead of varying by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and thus implementing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+anomaly+cancellation"} Green-Schwarz anomaly cancellation
            constraint, it is equivalent to first vary with respect to the other fields, and then insert the resulting equations in terms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            into the action functional.
          %p
            Now since we are dealing with a twisted
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange equation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} of motion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi H
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  d a = \star H
                  \\,,
          %p
            hence the usual relation or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electro-magnetic+duality"} electro-magnetic duality
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrange+multiplier"} Lagrange multiplier
            now as the magentic dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            to the twisted
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/B-field"} B-field
          %p
            Plugging this algebraic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
            back into the above
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functional"} action functional
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            gives
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi S
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ˜
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mi d
                        %mi a
                        %mo ∧
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mi d
                        %mi a
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mtext kinetic action
                      %mrow
                        %mtext for axion field
                  %mo +
                  %munder
                    %munder
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∫
                          %mi X
                        %mi a
                        %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                        %mi tr
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mo ∇
                        %mo ∧
                        %msub
                          %mi F
                          %mo ∇
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⏟
                    %mfrac{:linethickness => "0"}
                      %mrow
                        %mtext axionic
                      %mrow
                        %mtext interaction
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tilde S
                  \=
                  \\underset{\text{kinetic action} \atop \text{for axion field}}{\underbrace{\int_X d a \wedge \star d a}}
                  +
                  \\underset{\text{axionic} \atop \text{interaction}}{\underbrace{\int_X a \, tr(F_\nabla \wedge F_\nabla)}}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This now is an action functional for an axion field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            of just the form required
            %a{:href => "#AsASolutionToTheStrongCPProblem"} above
            for the solution of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+CP-problem"} strong CP-problem
          %h3#StringyAxionPhenomenology Stringy axion phenomenology
          %p
            From
            = succeed ":" do
              %a{:href => "#AcharyaKaneKumar12"} Acharya, Kane &amp; Kumar 2012, p. 3
          %blockquote
            %p
              Now consider the axions. Due to the shift symmetries mentioned above, there are no
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
              contributions to their potential.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} Non-perturbative effects
              though, such as strong gauge dynamics, gauge
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            %p
              Furthermore, since there are large numbers of axions in general, their masses will essentially be uniformly distributed on a logarithmic scale (
              %a{:href => "#ADDKM09"}> 5
              ). See (
              %a{:href => "#AcharyaBobkovKumar10"}> 6
              ) for a detailed calculation of axion masses in string/M effective theories.
            %p
              Like the moduli, the axions are also very weakly coupled to matter and therefore do not thermalize in general. Moreover, since their masses are tiny - ranging from
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi m
                      %mrow
                        %mn 3
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m_{3/2}
              to even below the Hubble scale today - many of them, including the QCD axion, start coherent oscillations during the time that the moduli are dominating the energy density, but before BBN.
          %p
            Observe that ultralight axion fields is precisely what is required in HEP
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phenomenology"} phenomenology
            for
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                the solution to the
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+CP-problem"} strong CP-problem
            %li
              %p
                the solution of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
                problem via
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} BEC/superfluid/wave/fuzzy dark matter
                (e.g.
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a{:href => "#HOTW16"} Hui-Ostriker-Tremaine-Witten 16
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/axion+inflation"} axion inflation
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy dark matter
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+CP+problem"} strong CP problem
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G%E2%82%82-MSSM"} G₂-MSSM
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The axion as such was originally proposed in
          %ul
            %li#Weinberg77
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steven+Weinberg"}> Steven Weinberg
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em New light boson
            %li#Wilczek78
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Wilczek"}> Frank Wilczek
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Problem of strong P and T invariance in the presence of instantons
          %p The experimentally viable variant as the “invisible axion” is due to
          %ul
            %li#DineFischlerSrednicki81
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Dine"}> Michael Dine
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Willy+Fischler"} Willy Fischler
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Srednicki"} Mark Srednicki
              = succeed "," do
                %em A simple solution to the strong CP problem with a harmless axion
          %p
            The observation that the “invisible axion” is a candidate for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            is due to three groups:
          %ul
            %li#PreskillWiseWilczek83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Preskill"}> John Preskill
                , M. Wise,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Wilczek"} Frank Wilczek
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Cosmology of the invisible axion
            %li#AbbottSikivie83
              %p
                L.F. Abbott, P. Sikivie,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A Cosmological Bound on the Invisible Axion
            %li#DineFischler83
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Dine"}> Michael Dine
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Willy+Fischler"} Willy Fischler
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Not So Harmless Axion
          %p A historical recollection of the development until here is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Wilczek"}> Frank Wilczek
              ,
              %em Birth of axions
              in
              %em This Week’s Citation Classic
              (1991) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.garfield.library.upenn.edu/classics1991/A1991FE76900001.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            The argument that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+Yang-Mills+theory"} topological
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interaction
            term
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∝
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟨
                  %mi F
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ⟩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \propto a \langle F \wedge F\rangle
            gives the axion field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            a vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+expectation+value"} vacuum expectation value
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#VafaWitten84
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cumrun+Vafa"}> Cumrun Vafa
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em Parity Conservation in Quantum Chromodynamics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://journals.aps.org/prl/abstract/10.1103/PhysRevLett.53.535"} publisher
          %p
            A reformulation of this effect in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+forms"} Chern-Simons forms
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gia+Dvali"}> Gia Dvali
              ,
              %em Three-Form Gauging of axion Symmetries and Gravity
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0507215"}> arXiv:hep-th/0507215
              )
          %p Review:
          %ul
            %li#KusterRaffeltBeltran08
              %p
                Markus Kuster, Georg Raffelt, Berta Beltrán (eds.),
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Axions: Theory, cosmology, and Experimental Searches
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-73518-2_2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-540-73518-2_2
            %li
              %p
                Jihn E. Kim, Gianpaolo Carosi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Axions and the Strong CP Problem
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0807.3125"} arXiv:0807.3125
            %li
              %p
                Masahiro Kawasaki, Kazunori Nakayama,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Axions : Theory and Cosmological Role
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1301.1123"} arXiv:1301.1123
            %li
              %p
                Luca Di Luzio, Maurizio Giannotti, Enrico Nardi, Luca Visinelli,
                %em The landscape of QCD axion models
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.01100"}> arXiv:2003.01100
                )
            %li
              %p
                Igor G. Irastorza,
                %em An introduction to axions and their detection
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.07376"}> arXiv:2109.07376
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Ib%C3%A1%C3%B1ez"}> Luis Ibáñez
                \:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Three-forms, and Axions: String and Particle Physics Applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Fayet Fest
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://moriond.in2p3.fr/Fayet/transparencies/ibanez.pdf"} pdf
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/files/Ibanez-Axions.pdf", :title => "pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axion"} Axion
          %h3#ReferencesInStringTheory In string theory
          %p
            Discussion of the various ways that axions naturally appear in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#SvrcekWitten06
              Peter Svrcek,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em Axions In String Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0605206"} arXiv:hep-th/0605206
          %p
            Specifically for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-theory"} F-theory
            sector of string theory:
          %ul
            %li#Grimm14
              Thomas Grimm,
              %em Axion Inflation in F-theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1404.4268"}> arXiv:1404.4268
              )
          %p Specifically open string axions
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriele+Honecker"}> Gabriele Honecker
              , section 4 of
              = succeed "," do
                %em From Type II string theory towards BSM/dark sector physics
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.00007"} arXiv:1610.00007
          %p
            A textbook account of axion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+phenomenology"} string phenomenology
            is in
          %ul
            %li#IbanezUranga12
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luis+Ib%C3%A1%C3%B1ez"}> Luis Ibáñez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Angel+Uranga"} Angel Uranga
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/String+Theory+and+Particle+Physics+--+An+Introduction+to+String+Phenomenology"} String Theory and Particle Physics – An Introduction to String Phenomenology
          %p
            Discussion of stringy axion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            (such as
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy dark matter
          %ul
            %li#ADDKM09
              %p
                Asimina Arvanitaki, Savas Dimopoulos, Sergei Dubovsky, Nemanja Kaloper, John March-Russell,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em String Axiverse
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0905.4720"} arXiv:0905.4720
            %li#AcharyaBobkovKumar10
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bobby+Acharya"}> Bobby Acharya
                , Konstantin Bobkov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Piyush+Kumar"} Piyush Kumar
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An M Theory Solution to the Strong CP Problem and Constraints on the Axiverse
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.5138"} arXiv:1004.5138
            %li#AcharyaKaneKumar12
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bobby+Acharya"}> Bobby Acharya
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gordon+Kane"} Gordon Kane
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Piyush+Kumar"} Piyush Kumar
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Compactified String Theories – Generic Predictions for Particle Physics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.2795"} arXiv:1204.2795
          %h3#in_holographic_qcd In holographic QCD
          %p
            Realization in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto+model"} Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto model
            for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
          %ul
            %li
              Francesco Bigazzi, Alessio Caddeo, Aldo L. Cotrone, Paolo Di Vecchia, Andrea Marzolla:
              %em The Holographic QCD Axion
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.12117"}> arXiv:1906.12117
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h3#experimental_signature Experimental signature
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            signatures of and constraints on axion physics:
          %h4#ReferencesExperimentalSignatureInParticlePhysics In particle physics
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            signatures of and constraints on axion physics from
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physics
          %p
            Specifically contribution of axions to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anomalous+magnetic+moment"} anomalous magnetic moment
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electron"} electron
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/muon"} muon
            in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QED"} QED
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Yannis Semertzidis,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Magnetic and Electric Dipole Moments in Storage Rings
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Axions: Theory, cosmology, and Experimental Searches
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "#KusterRaffeltBeltran08"} Kuster-Raffelt-Beltran 08
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-73518-2_2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-540-73518-2_2
            %li
              %p
                Roberta Armillis, Claudio Coriano’, Marco Guzzi, Simone Morelli,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Axions and Anomaly-Mediated Interactions: The Green-Schwarz and Wess-Zumino Vertices at Higher Orders and g-2 of the muon
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1882"} arXiv:0808.1882
            %li
              %p
                W.J. Marciano, A. Masiero, P. Paradisi, M. Passera,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Contributions of axion-like particles to lepton dipole moments
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1607.01022"} arXiv:1607.01022
            %li
              %p
                Martin Bauer, Matthias Neubert, Andrea Thamm,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Collider Probes of Axion-Like Particles
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1708.00443"} arXiv:1708.00443
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/JHEP12(2017)044"} doi:10.1007/JHEP12(2017)044
          %p
            The basic relevant
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagrams"} Feynman diagrams
            are worked out here:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://www-personal.umich.edu/~jbourj/peskin/6-3.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frank+Wilczek"}> Frank Wilczek
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em New Ideas in Axion Searches
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://frankwilczek.com/2017/axion_searches_01.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            For the
            %a{:href => "http://abracadabra.mit.edu/"} ABRACADABRA
            (A Broadband/Resonant Approach to Cosmic Axion Detection with an Amplifying B-field Ring Apparatus) approach to axion detection, see
          %ul
            %li
              Jonathan Ouellet,
              = succeed "," do
                %em ABRACADABRA: A Broadband Search for Axion Dark Matter
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://indico.fnal.gov/event/13702/session/3/contribution/45/material/slides/0.pdf"} pdf
          %p For the ADMX experiment:
          %ul
            %li#Avignone18
              Frank T. Avignone III,
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://physics.aps.org/articles/v11/34"} Homing in on Axions?
          %h4#in_astrophysics In astrophysics
          %p
            Axion signatures in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravitational+waves"} gravitational waves
            potentially visible by
            = succeed "-type" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/LIGO"} LIGO
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiments"} experiments
            are discussed in
          %ul
            %li#HJSSZ18
              Junwu Huang, Matthew C. Johnson, Laura Sagunski, Mairi Sakellariadou, Jun Zhang,
              %em Prospects for axion searches with Advanced LIGO through binary mergers
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.02133"}> arXiv:1807.02133
              )
          %h4#in_cosmology In cosmology
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimental
            signatures of and constraints on axion physics in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"} fuzzy
            = succeed "-candidate:" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Joseph P. Conlon, M.C. David Marsh,
                %em Searching for a 0.1-1 keV Cosmic Axion Background
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1305.3603"}> arXiv:1305.3603
                )
              %blockquote
                %p
                  Primordial decays of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stabilization"} moduli
                  at
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi z
                        %mo ∼
                        %msup
                          %mn 10
                          %mn 12
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} z \sim 10^{12}
                  naturally generate a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+radiation"} dark radiation
                  Cosmic Axion Background (CAB) with
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 0.1
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                        %mi keV
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0.1 - 1 keV
                  energies. This CAB can be detected through axion-
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/photon"} photon
                  conversion in astrophysical
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/magnetic+fields"} magnetic fields
                  to give quasi-thermal excesses in the extreme ultraviolet and soft X-ray bands. Substantial and observable luminosities may be generated even for axion-photon couplings
                  = succeed "." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ≪
                          %msup
                            %mn 10
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 11
                          %msup
                            %mi GeV
                            %mrow
                              %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                              %mn 1
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ll 10^{-11} GeV^{-1}
          %p
            Discussion of the axion as a candidate for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+matter"} dark matter
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fuzzy+dark+matter"}> fuzzy dark matter
            ):
          %ul
            %li#HOTW16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lam+Hui"}> Lam Hui
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jeremiah+Ostriker"} Jeremiah Ostriker
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Tremaine"} Scott Tremaine
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"} Edward Witten
              = succeed "," do
                %em On the hypothesis that cosmological dark matter is composed of ultra-light bosons
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.08297"} arXiv:1610.08297
          %p and with its experimental bounds
          %ul
            %li
              Nilanjan Banik, Adam J. Christopherson, Pierre Sikivie, Elisa Maria Todarello,
              = succeed "," do
                %em New astrophysical bounds on ultralight axionlike particles
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1701.04573"} arXiv:1701.04573
          %p
            and as a candidate for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dark+energy"} dark energy
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stephon+Alexander"}> Stephon Alexander
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Brandenberger"} Robert Brandenberger
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Juerg+Froehlich"} Juerg Froehlich
              %em Tracking Dark Energy from Axion-Gauge Field Couplings
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1601.00057"}> arXiv:1601.00057
              )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Luca Visinelli:
              %em Axions as Dark Matter, Dark Energy, and Dark Radiation
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.17059"}> arXiv:2509.17059
              \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
